> API and interpretation

Integer-valued outputs remain floating-point scalars; no narrowing to an integer
type occurs. Ties refer to the original represented input. Signed zero inputs
propagate unchanged. Sample summaries validate all observations before rounding
and accumulate errors in widened arithmetic. Positive-only ties deliberately
expose a direction bias that need not generalize to another sample.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

> References

- [IEEE754 floating-point arithmetic standard|https://doi.org/10.1109/IEEESTD.2019.8766229]

